+# Decision: Reconciliation of concurrent visit edits
+
+## Context
+
+A coastal conservation team records about 6,000 plot visits per season on shared tablets that can be offline for 48 hours. Workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes, so a submission can be derived from a revision that is no longer current by the time it reaches the server. Arrival order is not evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative. Two coordinators resolve questions about submitted observations before a reviewed CSV is sent to a regional science partner each Friday.
+
+The HLD assigns the concurrency mechanism to the record-store node: this decision is owned there, and tablet-sync and review-export consume its contract rather than defining their own reconciliation semantics. Both candidate policies retain every original submission; the standing constraint that no submitted edit be discarded does not by itself separate them.
+
+A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated (correcting an observer name and adding a missing species); others are related (revising a species identification together with its cover estimate). The field schema does not encode which fields are scientifically related, so the server cannot infer those relationships from field names alone.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Selection rests on weighted criteria; no gate eliminates a credible option.
+
+- **Scientific integrity under silent combination** — **major**. Whether the policy can produce a current record in which two scientifically related edits were combined without a human noticing. Importance derives from the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Coordinator review workload** — **major**. How many visits reach the two coordinators for manual resolution, and whether that stays operable without a dedicated systems administrator. Importance derives from the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Preservation of whole-visit intent** — **moderate**. Whether a submitted visit remains a coherent scientific unit rather than being disassembled into field-level fragments. Importance derives from the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** — **moderate**. The amount of reconciliation logic that must be authored, tested, and maintained as the visit schema evolves.
+- **Submission size and offline footprint** — **minor**. What a tablet must retain and transmit per edit. Importance derives from the tenet *Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity*.
+
+A latent criterion worth naming: how readily a future team could **reverse** the choice if the review queue grows. A policy that is cheap to maintain but expensive to unwind should be weighed, because the conditions that make a large review workload acceptable may change.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission names the base revision from which the edit was derived and carries the worker's complete visit, not a field delta. When concurrent submissions are derived from the same base revision, the server retains each as a competing complete revision, all originals kept intact. Nothing is combined automatically. A coordinator reviews the competing revisions in a comparison view and establishes the visit's canonical revision; while a visit has unresolved competing revisions it is not export-ready. Submission metadata is a single base revision ID, so a tablet retains and transmits little per edit.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A and worker B both fetch revision 3 of a visit. A corrects an observer name; B revises a species identification and its cover estimate. Both submit, naming revision 3 as their base. The store holds revision 3 plus two competing complete revisions. A coordinator compares them and establishes the canonical revision; both workers' submissions remain in history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific integrity under silent combination: **strong** — no fields are combined by the server, so a scientifically related pair cannot be merged unnoticed; any combination is a human act that remains inspectable.
+- Coordinator review workload: **weak** — even disjoint edits create review work, so the queue includes visits that a merge could have resolved automatically.
+- Preservation of whole-visit intent: **strong** — each submission stays a complete, attributable visit.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong** — the policy needs no per-field merge rules and none to maintain as the schema grows.
+- Submission size and offline footprint: **strong** — a base revision ID and one complete visit, rather than a base snapshot plus structured changes.
+
+**Summary.** Maximum integrity and minimum machinery, paid for with the largest manual review queue.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission supplies its base state as a snapshot and a set of structured field changes. The server automatically combines changes to disjoint fields into a new current record and routes only overlapping changes to review. Maintained rules define combination for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Only genuinely conflicting edits create review work, so far more visits become export-ready without coordinator action. A combined record may be required to pass manual approval before export.
+
+**Demonstration.** In the same scenario, A's observer-name correction and B's species/cover revision touch disjoint fields, so the server combines them into one current record with no review item; the contributed field changes are recorded as provenance.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific integrity under silent combination: **weak** — disjoint field names can still be scientifically related (a species identification and its cover estimate are different fields but one observation); automatic combination can join such a pair without a human noticing, and the field schema gives the server no way to detect the relationship. Requiring manual approval of every combined record mitigates this at the cost of the workload advantage.
+- Coordinator review workload: **strong** — most concurrent edits resolve automatically, keeping the queue small.
+- Preservation of whole-visit intent: **partial** — a current record can be assembled from fragments of several workers' visits.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak** — combination rules must be authored, tested, and maintained per field type, and revisited whenever the schema changes.
+- Submission size and offline footprint: **strong** — a base snapshot plus structured changes is comparable to a complete visit at this scale, and the tablet must retain the base state.
+
+**Summary.** Minimum review workload, paid for with maintained merge rules and a residual risk of joining scientifically related edits unnoticed.
+
+**Why this option remains credible.** It dominates on the two workload criteria, and its central risk is mitigable in principle: a team could require manual approval of every combined record, or encode the scientific relationships the field schema omits. Two conditions would make it the better choice on re-running this judgment: a review queue grown beyond what two coordinators can operate, or a relationship model reliable enough that automatic combination no longer risks unnoticed joins. Both are contingent, not present facts, which is why it is recorded here as a live alternative rather than dismissed.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions is selected.**
+
+It wins on the criteria weighted **major** and on preservation of whole-visit intent, decisively on scientific integrity: because the server never combines fields, a scientifically related pair of edits cannot be joined silently, and the path from a submitted observation to the exported value stays inspectable to the science partner. It loses on coordinator review workload, and that is accepted explicitly — the team takes a larger review queue over maintained merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing. The decision **turns on** the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and is **taken against** *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*: an integrity requirement overrides the workload preference here. The offline tenet is served incidentally, since a base revision ID is cheaper to retain than a base snapshot.
+
+**What it forecloses.** Automatic combination of disjoint field edits, field-level provenance of a combined record, and any maintained merge-rule set. These are not deferred features of this design; they are the alternative's mechanism.
+
+**What it obligates downstream.**
+
+- *record-store* — the submission envelope carries a base revision ID; concurrent submissions become competing complete revisions; retrieval distinguishes a resolved canonical revision from unresolved review work and names the competing revision IDs; a resolution operation records the coordinator and the reviewed submissions; no field combination is performed.
+- *tablet-sync* (cascade obligation) — retain the base revision ID a draft was derived from, submit complete visit revisions rather than field deltas, surface pending competing revisions to field workers, and size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope.
+- *review-export* (cascade obligation) — provide a revision comparison view for coordinators, hold unresolved visits out of the CSV, and export only resolved canonical revisions.
+
+Those two nodes are sized around this contract and link back to it; they do not define their own reconciliation semantics. Their designs are separate work.
+
+**Revisit condition.** If the review queue grows beyond what two coordinators can operate, or a reliable model of scientific field relationships becomes available, re-run this judgment; the field-aware option remains a credible alternative.
